<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cape-dutch">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cape Dutch</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cape <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> was the form of Dutch spoken at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> by settlers, the enslaved and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe</a> communities from the 17th century. Simplified and influenced by Malay, Portuguese and Khoekhoe, it gradually developed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a>. Frontier groups such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oorlam">Oorlam</a> and Nama adopted it along with horses and guns, carrying it far north of the colony.</p></div>
